             774                     !
             775                     ! compute diffusion coefficients based on local scheme above pbl
             776                     !>  ## Compute diffusion coefficients above the PBL top
             777                     !!  Diffusion coefficients above the PBL top are computed as a function of local stability (gradient Richardson number), shear, and a length scale from Louis (1979) \cite louis_1979 :
             778                     !!  \f[
             779                     !!  K_{m,h}=l^2f_{m,h}(Ri_g)\left|\frac{\partial U}{\partial z}\right|
             780                     !!  \f]
             781                     !!  The functions used (\f$f_{m,h}\f$) depend on the local stability. First, the gradient Richardson number is calculated as
             782                     !!  \f[
             783                     !!  Ri_g=\frac{\frac{g}{T}\frac{\partial \theta_v}{\partial z}}{\frac{\partial U}{\partial z}^2}
             784                     !!  \f]
             785                     !!  where \f$U\f$ is the horizontal wind. For the unstable case (\f$Ri_g < 0\f$), the Richardson number-dependent functions are given by
             786                     !!  \f[
             787                     !!  f_h(Ri_g) = 1 + \frac{8\left|Ri_g\right|}{1 + 1.286\sqrt{\left|Ri_g\right|}}\\
             788                     !!  \f]
             789                     !!  \f[
             790                     !!  f_m(Ri_g) = 1 + \frac{8\left|Ri_g\right|}{1 + 1.746\sqrt{\left|Ri_g\right|}}\\
             791                     !!  \f]
             792                     !!  For the stable case, the following formulas are used
             793                     !!  \f[
             794                     !!  f_h(Ri_g) = \frac{1}{\left(1 + 5Ri_g\right)^2}\\
             795                     !!  \f]
             796                     !!  \f[
             797                     !!  Pr = \frac{K_h}{K_m} = 1 + 2.1Ri_g
             798                     !!  \f]
             799                     !!  The source for the formulas used for the Richardson number-dependent functions is unclear. They are different than those used in Hong and Pan (1996) \cite hong_and_pan_1996 as the previous documentation suggests. They follow equation 14 of Louis (1979
             800                     !!  \f[
             801                     !!  \frac{1}{l} = \frac{1}{kz} + \frac{1}{l_0}\\
             802                     !!  \f]
             803                     !!  \f[
             804                     !!  or\\
             805                     !!  \f]
             806                     !!  \f[
             807                     !!  l=\frac{l_0kz}{l_0+kz}
             808                     !!  \f]
             809                     !!  where \f$l_0\f$ is currently 30 m for stable conditions and 150 m for unstable. Finally, the diffusion coefficients are kept in a range bounded by the background diffusion and the maximum allowable values.
                                   <<< Loop-information Start >>>
